# AGENTS.md — recommended gigacode workflow guidance

Copy the sections below into your project's `AGENTS.md` (or equivalent agent
instructions file) when the `gigacode` MCP server is configured. Identical
text works for any MCP agent that names tools as `<server>_<tool>`.

---

## Code tools

A `gigacode` MCP server is connected. Its default coding tools are
`gigacode_code_find` and `gigacode_code_edit`; additional capabilities can be
discovered with `gigacode_tool_search` and invoked with `gigacode_tool_call`.

For multi-file work, start with `gigacode_get_task_context(task)`. For a known
symbol, use `gigacode_code_navigate(action="symbol", file=..., symbol=...,
include_source=true)` to read only its body without embedding the project.
Other actions expose structure, parents/children, callers/callees/references,
summaries and dependency graphs. Disambiguate using returned symbol IDs.
Discover read-only Git tools for changed files/symbols, diffs and history.
Context suggestions and syntactic edges are evidence, not proof of runtime
dispatch or a complete architecture. Do not guess unresolved targets.

Use them for locating and changing code instead of your built-in
grep/read/edit tools:

1. Call `gigacode_code_find` with a symbol, file name, or behavioral query.
   Inspect the declaration; behavioral results are candidates, not certainty.
   The working project is embedded automatically on first use.
2. Call `gigacode_code_edit` with the returned file, a unique `old_text` and its
   `new_text` replacement. Copy indentation and include context if repeated.
   Use `expected_hash=file_hash` when available; no narrower read is required.
   The returned hash can guard the next edit without another read.
   Alternatively, anchors plus `new_lines` replace the ENTIRE inclusive range,
   including declarations and decorators, and require `expected_hash`.
   The tool checks Python syntax before mutation and persists the change.
   `dry_run=true` previews without changing buffer or disk.
3. Run relevant tests. Formatting/linting with the discovered `post_edit`
   chain is optional. Do not rediscover ordinary coding tools.

## Rules

- Send native JSON argument objects, never XML tool-call wrappers or
  `arg_key`/`arg_value` delimiters. `old_text`/`new_text` are source strings,
  not serialized lists. XML belonging to source code is allowed.
- Never repeat an identical search call. If a result did not help, change the
  query wording, the `mode`, or read the specific file range directly.
- Do not use built-in grep/read/edit for code work while gigacode tools are
  available; use your built-in tools only if no `gigacode_*` tool is visible.
- Work only inside the project directory.
- Use returned root-relative paths. Either separator, project prefixes and
  in-root absolute paths also work; ambiguous basenames are not guessed.

## Recovery

- A stale hash/anchor requires a fresh read, not an identical retry.
- If disk changed externally, discover `reload_codebase` to resync, then
  re-read and reapply. Do not discard pending work without authorization.
- Direct edits block when unrelated files have pending buffer edits. Resolve
  those explicitly. Legacy buffered writes still require `commit`.
- Legacy deferred-only mode: discover `tool_chain`, use `anchor_read`, then
  `anchor_apply(dry_run=false)`. Replace its complete `editable_range`.
